## Previously raised findings (review-round registry)

These defects were raised on earlier review rounds of this PR. For each finding below: verify fixed (cite the fixing hunk), withdraw (reason required), or mark re-introduced. New claims need new evidence — a claim already on this list is answered, not re-raised.

| id | status | severity | file | claim |
|----|--------|----------|------|-------|
| F1 | open | IMPORTANT | js/unit-tests/test_sessionDelta.js | ⚠️ important it 2e live it verdict mirror contradicts the implementation for exhausted keys the mirror s verdict test sessiondelta js 1409 1414 classifies keys with clsof and then when both probed keys land in cls 4 exhausted falls through to the usable desc comparison — but fa order queue by quota orders the exhausted tail by severity ascending then nextresettime ascending fa sh 394 399 usable never enters the cls 4 comparison the offline suite pins exactly this behavior it 2j sev 1 keys first soonest refill before late refill reproduced with both comparators extracted verbatim and run on the same fixtures a below 5h floor usable 50 5h refill 2min fa sh first severity tie nextreset asc b below 5h floor usable 80 5h refill 5d mirror verdict b first usable 80 50 so when the opt in live it runs against a pool with two drained keys — precisely the state the tail rules in this ticket exist for — it 2e exits 1 with verdict b first first zai code key while fa sh behaved exactly as specified a triage run will then catch a correct reorder fix re derive the tail order in the mirror — severity asc then nextreset asc — before any usable tie break keep eq or unknown as the final fallback the derived map already returns severity nextreset in fa sh the mirror s derive computes nextreset but drops it from verdict |
| F2 | open | SUGGESTION | scripts/providers/fa.sh | 💡 suggestion lead rate … h in the digest shows tokens per 6 minutes under a h label 10× under rate rw max tw now 1 is tokens per millisecond fa sh 290 so tokens hour rate × 3 600 000 — but the digest prints math round m rate 360000 fa sh 371 i e tokens per 6 minutes labeled h reproduced against the real script fresh max key rw 140000 weekly reset 6d stderr digest zai code key lead rate 97 h printed by fa sh true hourly 140000 5184000ms × 3 600 000 972 2 h the pr s own it comments use the true hourly convention so the code can never print the numbers its tests cite it 2l comment rate 960 h would top the fefo ranking trap key 160 6000ms × 3 600 000 960 h the code prints 96 h it 2m comment beats the weekly 972 h code prints 97 h for that key ordering is unaffected — the factor cancels in rate vs rate comparisons — but this is the operator facing diagnostic ac3 s ref why digest and a lead key reporting 97 h when it actually burns 972 tokens hour misleads anyone tuning quota floor or triaging the pool fix math round m rate 3600000 and align the it 2m comment s 36000 h degraded figure which currently mixes conventions with the 972 h one next to it |

### How to answer

Add a `findingReview` block to `outputs/pr_review.json` (alongside `inlineComments`):

```json
{"findingReview": {
  "dispositions": [
    {"n": 1, "status": "fixed", "hunk": "<file:lines \u2014 the fixing hunk>"},
    {"n": 2, "status": "withdrawn", "reason": "<why this is not a defect>"},
    {"n": 3, "status": "re-introduced"}
  ],
  "confirmations": [
    {"proposedFor": 1, "same": true}
  ]
}}
```

Unconfirmed candidates become new identities (the merge bias is confirm-only); a genuinely new finding on a resolved item is expected to be raised again with new evidence.

diff --git a/scripts/providers/fa.sh b/scripts/providers/fa.sh
index 1d84822998..f9252393d9 100644
--- a/scripts/providers/fa.sh
+++ b/scripts/providers/fa.sh
@@ -368,7 +368,7 @@ _fa_order_queue_by_quota() {
         d.why = "exhausted:" + (m.below5 && m.belowW ? "5h+weekly" : (m.below5 ? "5h" : "weekly"));
       } else if (m.usable >= reserve) {
         d.cls = 1;
-        d.why = m.rate !== null ? "lead:rate=" + Math.round(m.rate * 360000) + "/h" : "lead:usable=" + m.usable;
+        d.why = m.rate !== null ? "lead:rate=" + Math.round(m.rate * 3600000) + "/h" : "lead:usable=" + m.usable;
       } else {
         d.cls = 2;
         d.why = "usable=" + m.usable;

# gh-1385 + gh-1534: quota-aware provider ordering — probe every zai queue
# entry's key ONCE against the coding-plan quota endpoint and re-order the
# queue FEFO-rate-first (gh-1534). Per key derive R5/RW (5-hour / weekly
# remaining), cap5/capW (the key's OWN plan capacity = remaining + usage —
# plan tier is never hardcoded, mixed max/cheap pools need no config) and
# tW (time to weekly reset). Score = RW / tW descending — FEFO for quota:
# burn the budget that refreshes soonest first, keep freshly refreshed
# balances intact. Percentage floors relative to the key's OWN capacity
# (QUOTA_FLOOR_5H_PCT=0.10, QUOTA_FLOOR_WEEKLY_PCT=0.02) demote keys that
# are functionally exhausted for their tier to a severity- then refill-
# ordered tail, while ranking stays in absolute tokens (rate, usable) so a
# cheap key's 200-token remainder can never outrank a max key's 2800.
# Lead tier = keys whose min(R5, RW) covers the derived reserve 0.75 x
# max(cap5) across the queue (no env knob — scales itself down for cheap-
# only pools); above-floor keys that cannot cover a leg fill after the
# leader by usable desc (failover filler); if NO key is leg-fit the gates
# are transparent — the most usable above-floor key takes the lead slot,
# the gates order the queue, they never block a leg. Functionally
# exhausted keys order by severity (below both windows' floors ranks below
# below-just-one) then nextResetTime ascending (soonest refill first — the
# best mid-leg failover ticket); 401/403 rank below the exhausted tail
# (static order); every other probe failure (non-200, timeout,
# unparseable/changed shape, missing curl/node) keeps the static position
# and NEVER fails the leg — the endpoint is undocumented, its shape can
# change without notice. A missing weekly row degrades to 5h-only scoring
